{
  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "rp-03-comparing-and-transforming-ratios",
  "version": 1,
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "entryNodeId": "start",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Comparing and Transforming Ratios",
    "description": "Compare ratios and use compounded, duplicate, triplicate, and componendo-dividendo relationships accurately.",
    "teachingApproach": "Build the ratio model from the quantities named, explain why each transformation preserves or changes it, show intermediate steps and checks, and use fresh practice with targeted remedial retries.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 45,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 13 “Ratio and Proportion,” printed pages 426–474. Approved scope: Compare a:b and c:d by cross-products only after aligning units and positive denominators. Form compounded ratios by multiplying corresponding terms; derive duplicate/triplicate and sub-duplicate/sub-triplicate ratios with domain care. Explain componendo and dividendo from a/b=c/d: (a+b)/(a−b)=(c+d)/(c−d), when denominators are non-zero, and show when this transformation is useful. Use exact equivalent forms and verify transformed equalities algebraically. Ground in the chapter’s Important Concepts and solved examples 9, 11, 13, and the relevant objective patterns."
  },
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Comparing and Transforming Ratios: Compare ratios and use compounded, duplicate, triplicate, and componendo-dividendo relationships accurately. This lesson builds the ratio model, works through its reasoning, then checks understanding with fresh practice and targeted recovery.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Equivalent transformations start from a known equality a/b=c/d. Their validity depends on non-zero denominators and on preserving the same operations on both sides.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_2"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_2": {
      "id": "teach_2",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "The compounded ratio of a:b and c:d is ac:bd. It compares the product of the first terms with the product of the second terms; simplify the resulting pair only after multiplying.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_3"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_3": {
      "id": "teach_3",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "The duplicate ratio of a:b is a²:b² and the triplicate ratio is a³:b³. Sub-duplicate and sub-triplicate ratios use square and cube roots termwise, so non-negative terms are assumed for ordinary ratios.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_4"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_4": {
      "id": "teach_4",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "For example, compounding 3:4 with 5:7 gives 15:28. The ratios must represent compatible comparisons; check the resulting terms and units before treating the compound as meaningful.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_5"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_5": {
      "id": "teach_5",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "If a/b=c/d, adding 1 to both sides yields (a+b)/b=(c+d)/d (componendo). Subtracting 1 yields (a−b)/b=(c−d)/d (dividendo), where denominators permit.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_6"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_6": {
      "id": "teach_6",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Dividing the componendo equation by the dividendo equation gives (a+b)/(a−b)=(c+d)/(c−d), when differences are non-zero. The result follows from the original equality; it is not a separate memorized trick.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_7"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_7": {
      "id": "teach_7",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Ratio comparison can use cross-products: compare ad with bc for a/b and c/d. This is exact for positive denominators and avoids misleading rounded decimals.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_8"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_8": {
      "id": "teach_8",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Common errors include squaring only one term, adding corresponding terms instead of multiplying in a compounded ratio, and using componendo-dividendo when a difference is zero.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_9"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_9": {
      "id": "teach_9",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Check any transformation by substituting a simple ratio. If 2:3 equals 4:6, its duplicate ratios 4:9 and 16:36 are equal; compounding 2:3 with 4:6 gives 8:18, also equivalent.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_10"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_10": {
      "id": "teach_10",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Keep ratio order consistent. Compounding 2:3 with 5:7 is 10:21; reversing the second ratio first would instead answer a different comparison.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_11"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_11": {
      "id": "teach_11",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A transformation may create a denominator such as a−b. State the condition a≠b before dividing; cross-multiplication alone may remain valid when that difference is zero.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_12"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_12": {
      "id": "teach_12",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Use the simplest operation that preserves the information. Cross-products solve most comparison questions; componendo and dividendo are useful when sums or differences are explicitly involved.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_13"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_13": {
      "id": "teach_13",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "When terms are fractions, clear their denominators with a common factor before compounding or comparing. This keeps arithmetic manageable and preserves equivalence.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_14"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_14": {
      "id": "teach_14",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A final check should return to the original ratios, not just the transformed expression. Confirm that each step used reversible operations under the stated non-zero conditions.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_15"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_15": {
      "id": "teach_15",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "You should now be able to compare ratios, form compounded and power ratios, and derive sum/difference transformations with their conditions stated.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "focus_1"
        }
      ]
    },
